            "target_entity_instance": "Lena River",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "On the other hand, looking at the long-term trend, a drying trend was detected in the lower basin of the Lena River (the tundra region along the Arctic Ocean coast), and since 2002, a drying trend of more than 6 mm per year in the water column height has been observed (Suzuki et al. 2016).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "heatwaves and drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Drought",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "However, another opposing effect of warming is the impact associated with heatwaves and drought (Dobricic et al. 2020).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Wildfires",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Wildfire",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "Wildfires also play a significant role in the rapid thawing of permafrost (Sizov et al. 2021).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CHANGE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "rainfall contribution",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Park et al. (2026) extended and applied this model to the pan-Arctic region, simulating the component separation of origin water in evapotranspiration for the period 1979\u20132016 (Fig. 7). Rainfall and snowmelt accounted for 67% of annual evapotranspiration, showing an interannual increase in rainfall contribution (Fig. 7f).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
